# Rule - Common Test Strategy

## Purpose

This is the **single baseline** test strategy for all projects. It defines the minimum pyramid, practices, and quality bar.

Do **not** duplicate this content in a separate always-on rule (e.g. legacy `test-strategy.mdc`). One baseline avoids conflicting instructions.

Project-specific test strategies (e.g. `specs/test-strategy.md`) may **extend** this baseline with domain fixtures, tools, latency budgets, and journeys. They must **not** weaken it.

## Precedence & compliance

1. **Always apply** this common strategy.
2. If a project defines its own test strategy or quality standards, **also** apply that document in full.
3. Project-specific rules must **comply with** this common strategy:
   - They may add stricter coverage, extra environments, or more journeys
   - They must not remove pyramid layers, skip required gates, or lower the quality checklist below
4. If the project has **no** test strategy, use this document alone (including the quality checklist).
5. Conflicts: choose the **stricter** interpretation; ask the user if still ambiguous.

When creating or editing a project test strategy, state explicitly that it extends `common-test-strategy` and list any stricter deltas.

## Test pyramid

Target mix (adjust only with user approval):

| Layer | Share | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unit / component | ~70% | Fast, isolated, many cases |
| Integration / contract | ~20% | Module boundaries, APIs, DB, auth |
| E2E (real browser) | ~10% | Few critical user journeys |

Prefer many cheap unit tests over duplicating the same path only in E2E.

## Unit & component tests

### Scope

- Business logic and non-trivial helpers must have unit tests
- Critical paths: **100%** coverage of the changed critical path
- Overall coverage target: **≥ 80%** where the stack supports measurement (or project-stricter)

### Principles

- One behavior per test
- Name: `should_[expected]_when_[condition]` (or equivalent clarity)
- AAA: Arrange → Act → Assert
- Independent, order-independent, deterministic

### Mocks

**Prefer real / test doubles that preserve behavior:**

- Test DB or isolated data dir over silent no-op mocks for persistence
- Test/fixture APIs over inventing success payloads in production code paths

**Mock when necessary:**

- Paid / irreversible third parties (payments, SMS) unless an approved sandbox exists
- Time (`Date.now`), randomness
- Filesystem only when isolation requires it

Do not ship features that only work against mocks unless the user explicitly allows it.

## Integration / contract tests

- Cover API request/response contracts, CRUD, authn/authz, frontend–backend wiring
- Use isolated test data; clean up or reset between tests
- Include error and partial-failure paths
- Default CI: fixtures / sandboxes; live keys only in opt-in jobs

## E2E & web app testing

- Run in a real browser (Playwright or equivalent)
- Cover critical journeys: auth, core happy path, at least one failure path
- Drive UI like a user; assert on visible outcomes
- Wait for rendered state (`networkidle` or an explicit selector) before inspect/assert
- Prefer stable selectors: `role`, accessible name / `text`, test id — not layout-brittle CSS
- Avoid hardcoded sleeps unless unavoidable
- Static HTML: `file://` is fine; dynamic apps: start the real server (e.g. `with_server` / `npm run dev`) then automate

## Test data

- Separate test DB / temp data dir; never use production data
- Per-test isolation via fixtures, factories, transactions, or reset
- Clean up after runs to avoid pollution

## TDD workflow

1. **Red** — write a failing test that states the behavior  
2. **Green** — minimal code to pass  
3. **Refactor** — clean up while green  

Commit when green when the project workflow expects it. Do not expand scope mid-cycle without capturing a new story.

## CI

- Tests run on every push / PR
- Failures block merge
- Publish coverage (or equivalent) when available
- Default pipeline stays fixture-only / non-destructive

## When tests may be skipped

**Never skip for:**

- Business logic, API endpoints, data transforms, authn/authz

**May skip (document why):**

- Pure presentational components with no logic
- Config-only and type-only files

## Failure handling

- Prefer fixing production code over weakening the test
- Fix a wrong test; never delete or comment out a failing test to “go green”
- Never lower the quality bar to pass CI

Debug: read failure → clarify expected behavior → check implementation → fix → re-run full relevant suite.

## Quality standard checklist

Meet **all applicable** items before marking work done (DoD “Quality met”). Project strategies may add items; they may not drop these without user approval.

### Functional

- [ ] Core user path works end-to-end in a real browser (not only unit tests)
- [ ] Happy path and at least one failure / empty / loading state are verified
- [ ] Form validation and error messages are correct and visible (where UI exists)
- [ ] Auth-gated routes/APIs reject unauthenticated or unauthorized access correctly

### Automation & E2E

- [ ] Wait for rendered state before assert/interact
- [ ] Selectors are stable (`role` / `text` / test id)
- [ ] Critical journey covered by E2E automation or an evidenced manual browser script
- [ ] No unnecessary hardcoded timing sleeps

### UX & accessibility (UI work)

- [ ] Desktop and mobile viewports checked for changed screens
- [ ] Keyboard: focus order, Escape closes dialogs, primary actions reachable
- [ ] Status/meaning is not color-only
- [ ] No layout overflow / clipped controls on target breakpoints

### Data & honesty

- [ ] UI/API expose real or approved fixture data — no fabricated domain facts
- [ ] Loading / partial-failure states do not blank the whole experience
- [ ] Secrets never appear in client storage, responses meant for the browser, or logs

### Performance

- [ ] Primary flows feel responsive (no multi-second freezes on common actions)
- [ ] Load/navigation within agreed budget or no clear regression vs baseline
- [ ] Large lists use pagination, virtualization, or equivalent
- [ ] Expensive calls are not duplicated on remount/re-render without need
- [ ] Loading indicators for slow async work; UI remains usable

### Security

- [ ] Authorization enforced on server/BFF; UI hiding is not the control
- [ ] No secrets in client code, `localStorage`, client-exposed cookies, or logs
- [ ] Input and URL params validated/sanitized; no XSS via rendered HTML
- [ ] Mutations use CSRF-safe patterns where applicable
- [ ] Errors do not leak stacks, tokens, or internals
- [ ] Provider failures degrade safely without exposing credentials

### Regression gate

- [ ] Related unit / integration / E2E tests pass in CI (fixture-only by default)
- [ ] No unexpected console/server errors on the verified path
- [ ] Coverage/quality targets met for the change (critical path 100%; overall ≥ 80% or project-stricter)
- [ ] Screenshots or short notes attached when AC requires visual proof

## How to apply

**Before coding**

- Write or update tests that define expected behavior
- Check for a project `test-strategy` and apply both that and this baseline

**During development**

- Stay in Red–Green–Refactor; keep the suite green
- Prefer real integrations over mocks unless allowed

**When adding a project strategy**

- Start from this baseline; document extensions only
- Keep or raise the quality checklist; never silently drop items

**At review / DoD**

- Verify pyramid coverage for the change
- Complete the quality checklist (plus any project-specific extras)
